{"type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"tool_use_id":"toolu_01EkRYvvLddTBrjUBHhNNuw8","type":"tool_result","content":"499\t(2)~Bias should be assessed per axis rather than as a monolithic ``style'' factor, since different axes produce different effect sizes and interact differently with different judges.\n500\t\n501\t\\paragraph{Limitations.}\n502\tCSD requires access to judge outputs (though not internals), limiting applicability to fully closed systems.\n503\tOur three-judge, three-benchmark scope, while substantially broader than prior single-setting studies, does not cover all judges or evaluation scenarios.\n504\tThe $n=100$ per cell design provides adequate power for the observed effect sizes but may miss smaller effects.\n505\tWe test three style axes; additional axes (e.g., emotional valence, technical density) remain unexplored.\n506\t\n507\t% ============================================================\n508\t% 7. CONCLUSION\n509\t% ============================================================\n510\t\\section{Conclusion}\n511\t\n512\tWe presented Causal Style Decomposition (CSD), a black-box framework for detecting per-axis causal style bias in LLM judges.\n513\tThrough systematic experiments across 3 judges, 3 benchmarks, and 3 style axes, we established three findings:\n514\t(1)~LLM judges exhibit significant and consistent style premiums that CSD reliably detects;\n515\t(2)~standard Likert scoring systematically misses these biases, with 7/9 judge$\\times$benchmark combinations showing a measurement gap where only pairwise comparison achieves significance;\n516\t(3)~style bias is axis-dependent, with an effect ordering (formality $>$ register $>$ tone) that reflects the linguistic breadth of each transformation.\n517\tRobustness is confirmed through scaled replication ($n=500$, $p<10^{-6}$), original-preference controls, and rewriter ablation.\n518\t\n519\tThese results urge the community to adopt pairwise comparison for style bias auditing and to move beyond single-axis analyses of judge reliability.\n520\t\n521\t\\end{document}\n522\t"}]},"parent_tool_use_id":"toolu_01XyEJot4dvSG7bL5tvv1urF","session_id":"e3a9c93d-dffd-441a-83b0-1831ff4a3a69","uuid":"8f53a507-bafb-47ec-9e6e-37b39e721293","timestamp":"2026-05-20T21:56:26.243Z"}
{"type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"tool_use_id":"toolu_01BeN4UFK3voEBVeAbBH5e9s","type":"tool_result","content":"{\"result\":\"{\\\"success\\\": true, \\\"data\\\": {\\\"path\\\": \\\"/home/ubuntu/.agent-ml-research-idea_gen_0514_5/projects/causal_style_decomp_judge/report.md\\\", \\\"content\\\": \\\"# Handover — 2026-05-20\\\\n\\\\n> 由 Agent 生成，每次状态变更后自动更新。硬限 150 行。\\\\n\\\\n## 运行中\\\\n（无）\\\\n\\\\n## 待处理\\\\n（无）\\\\n\\\\n## 近期结论\\\\n- **verbosity_32b** (negative): Verbosity axis (verbose/concise) produces stronger style bias signal than formality in GPT-4o judge: pairwise win rate ≥60% with p<0.05 and/or Likert corrected ATE with d≥0.3, because verbose responses contain more surface-level detail that judges may conflate with thoroughness ← 基于 pairwise_32b\\\\n- **pairwise_32b** (success): Likert 1-10 integer granularity masks a real formality preference in GPT-4o; position-controlled pairwise comparison (more sensitive measurement) reveals significant formal win rate >55% where Likert ATE was non-significant ← 基于 mvp_32b_ate\\\\n- **mvp_32b_ate** (negative): Qwen3-32B (stronger instruction following than 8B) produces higher-fidelity RATE counterfactuals with lower rewrite bias, revealing significant formality premium (ATE>0.3, d>0.3) in GPT-4o judge scores that was masked by 8B rewrite noise ← 基于 mvp_8b_ate\\\\n\\\\n## 趋势\\\\n成功 1 / 负向 3 / 失败 0 / 共 4\\\\n\\\\n## 下一步\\\\n（无活跃 idea，请通过 ideas.md 添加）\\\\n\\\\n## 资源状态\\\\nwestb-29275: 无 GPU 信息\\\\n\\\\n## 告警\\\\n（无）\\\\n\\\", \\\"total_chars\\\": 1086, \\\"truncated\\\": false}, \\\"error\\\": null, \\\"metadata\\\": {}}\"}"}]},"parent_tool_use_id":"toolu_01HHJivM5WhiGQkxWgoTKbUa","session_id":"e3a9c93d-dffd-441a-83b0-1831ff4a3a69","uuid":"09e542b4-57f9-45d5-9e9d-c2dc17473b5d","timestamp":"2026-05-20T21:56:25.812Z"}
