{"type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"tool_use_id":"toolu_01DT9hLPWd9QfbvMJNWYmzjE","type":"tool_result","content":"1\t\\documentclass[11pt,a4paper]{article}\n2\t\\usepackage[margin=1in]{geometry}\n3\t\\usepackage{times}\n4\t\\usepackage{latexsym}\n5\t\\usepackage{booktabs}\n6\t\\usepackage{amsmath}\n7\t\\usepackage{amssymb}\n8\t\\usepackage{graphicx}\n9\t\\usepackage{multirow}\n10\t\\usepackage{xcolor}\n11\t\\usepackage{tabularx}\n12\t\\usepackage[T1]{fontenc}\n13\t\\usepackage[utf8]{inputenc}\n14\t\\usepackage{hyperref}\n15\t\\usepackage{natbib}\n16\t\n17\t\\newcommand{\\cmark}{\\checkmark}\n18\t\\newcommand{\\xmark}{$\\times$}\n19\t\n20\t\\title{Causal Style Decomposition: Revealing Axis-Dependent Style Bias\\\\in LLM Judges via Counterfactual Pairwise Comparison}\n21\t\n22\t\\author{Anonymous}\n23\t\n24\t\\begin{document}\n25\t\\maketitle\n26\t\n27\t\\begin{abstract}\n28\tLLM-as-judge systems are increasingly used for evaluation and preference learning, yet recent work shows style bias dominates their assessments far more than position bias.\n29\tExisting approaches treat style as a monolithic dimension or rely on correlational evidence, leaving open the question of which specific style axes causally influence judge decisions and how to detect them.\n30\tWe propose \\textbf{Causal Style Decomposition (CSD)}, a black-box, post-hoc method that generates binary style counterfactuals---text pairs differing only along a single style axis---and measures judge bias via position-controlled pairwise comparison.\n31\tAcross three judges (GPT-4o, Claude-3.5-Sonnet, Gemini-2.0-Flash) and three benchmarks (LLMBar, AlpacaEval, MT-Bench), we find that:\n32\t(1) formality bias is significant and replicable on frontier judges ($p{<}0.001$ at $n{=}500$) but judge-dependent in magnitude;\n33\t(2) standard Likert scoring requires approximately $5{\\times}$ the sample size to detect the same bias that pairwise comparison reveals at $n{=}100$ ($p{=}0.042$ at $n{=}500$ vs.\\ $p{=}0.006$ at $n{=}100$), demonstrating that Likert systematically underestimates moderate style bias;\n34\t(3) bias varies across style axes---formality produces a clear premium, register shows borderline effects, tone is marginal, and verbosity fails content preservation entirely.\n35\tOur results provide actionable methodological guidance: researchers auditing LLM judges for style bias should use pairwise comparison rather than Likert scoring and analyze each style axis independently.\n36\t\\end{abstract}\n37\t\n38\t%--------------------------------------------------------------\n39\t\\section{Introduction}\n40\t\\label{sec:intro}\n41\t%--------------------------------------------------------------\n42\t\n43\tLLM-as-judge evaluation has become central infrastructure in NLP: it drives preference learning for RLHF~\\cite{ouyang2022training}, powers automatic benchmarks such as AlpacaEval~\\cite{dubois2024length} and MT-Bench~\\cite{zheng2024judging}, and increasingly replaces human annotation at scale.\n44\tRecent large-scale audits reveal a troubling pattern: style bias---not position bias---is the dominant systematic error in LLM judges, with correlation coefficients approaching $R{=}0.999$ between stylistic features and judge scores~\\cite{sosbench2025,raina2024llm}.\n45\t\n46\tDespite its severity, style bias remains poorly understood along two critical dimensions.\n47\tFirst, \\emph{which} specific style properties causally influence judge decisions? Existing work either treats style as a single variable (typically length)~\\cite{dubois2024length} or catalogs many features without causal identification~\\cite{casse2026}.\n48\tSecond, \\emph{how} should style bias be measured? The field relies heavily on Likert-scale pointwise scoring, yet its sensitivity to moderate-effect biases has not been systematically compared to alternatives.\n49\t\n50\tWe address both gaps with \\textbf{Causal Style Decomposition (CSD)}, a method that:\n51\t\\begin{enumerate}\n52\t    \\item Generates \\emph{binary style counterfactuals}---pairs of texts that differ only along a single style axis (e.g., formal vs.\\ casual) while preserving semantic content, verified via NLI and BERTScore;\n53\t    \\item Measures judge bias via \\emph{position-controlled pairwise comparison}, which we show is substantially more sensitive than Likert scoring for moderate effects;\n54\t    \\item Operates as a \\emph{black-box, post-hoc} audit requiring no access to judge internals or retraining.\n55\t\\end{enumerate}\n56\t\n57\tOur key findings, across 3 judges $\\times$ 3 benchmarks $\\times$ 4 style axes, are:\n58\t\n59\t\\noindent\\textbf{Measurement insight (C2).} Standard Likert scoring completely fails to detect formality bias at $n{=}100$ ($p{=}0.332$), while pairwise comparison on the same data is highly significant ($p{=}0.006$).\n60\tAt $n{=}500$, Likert barely reaches significance ($p{=}0.042$, $d{=}0.097$) while pairwise yields $p{<}0.001$ ($h{=}0.228$).\n61\tA researcher relying solely on Likert scoring would need approximately $5{\\times}$ the sample size to detect the same bias.\n62\t\n63\t\\noindent\\textbf{Detection method (C1).} CSD detects significant formality bias on two of three frontier judges (GPT-4o: 69.1\\%, $p{=}0.006$; Claude-3.5: 65.4\\%, $p{=}0.021$), with effect magnitude modulated by judge identity. Results replicate across an independent rewriter (Llama-3.1-70B, 67.4\\%, $p{=}0.009$) and survive original-preference correction on LLMBar.\n64\t\n65\t\\noindent\\textbf{Axis spectrum (C3).} Style bias is axis-dependent: formality is clearly detectable, register shows borderline significance ($p{=}0.048$ on GPT-4o), tone is marginal ($p{=}0.092$), and verbosity fails content preservation entirely (NLI 65\\%), establishing method boundaries. Effect magnitude tracks perceived formality/prestige.\n66\t\n67\t\n68\t%--------------------------------------------------------------\n69\t\\section{Related Work}\n70\t\\label{sec:related}\n71\t%--------------------------------------------------------------\n72\t\n73\t\\paragraph{Style Bias in LLM Judges.}\n74\tLength bias is the most studied style confounder in LLM evaluation. \\citet{dubois2024length} introduce length-controlled AlpacaEval, using regression to deconfound verbosity from quality.\n75\tSOS-Bench~\\cite{sosbench2025} injects content defects to measure judge sensitivity to style versus substance, finding style dominates across all tested judges.\n76\t\\citet{raina2024llm} and Preference Leakage~\\cite{preferenceleakage2026} document how style features in training data leak into judge preferences, inflating scores for responses matching training distributions.\n77\tCASSE~\\cite{casse2026} maps a $12{\\times}12$ style feature interaction matrix but relies on prompt engineering rather than counterfactual intervention, leaving causal confounds unresolved.\n78\t\n79\t\\paragraph{Causal Inference for Bias Detection.}\n80\tCounterfactual data augmentation has been effective for detecting social biases in NLP~\\cite{lu2020gender}, but has not been applied to style bias in LLM judges.\n81\tOur approach is closest in spirit to CausalBench~\\cite{causalbench2025}, which uses interventional data for benchmark evaluation, but we target judge bias rather than model capability.\n82\t\n83\t\\paragraph{Pairwise vs.\\ Pointwise Evaluation.}\n84\tThe relative merits of pairwise and pointwise (Likert) evaluation have been discussed in the context of human annotation~\\cite{kiritchenko2017best} and LLM judging~\\cite{zheng2024judging}, but no prior work has systematically compared their \\emph{sensitivity to style bias} using controlled counterfactual data.\n85\t\n86\t%--------------------------------------------------------------\n87\t\\section{Method: Causal Style Decomposition}\n88\t\\label{sec:method}\n89\t%--------------------------------------------------------------\n90\t\n91\tCSD consists of three stages: counterfactual generation, content verification, and bias measurement.\n92\t\n93\t\\subsection{Binary Style Counterfactual Generation}\n94\t\\label{sec:counterfactual}\n95\t\n96\tGiven a source text $x$ and a target style axis $a$ with two poles $\\{a^+, a^-\\}$ (e.g., formal/casual), we generate a style-transferred counterpart $x'$ using an instruction-tuned LLM as a rewriter.\n97\tThe rewriting prompt instructs the model to change only the surface style along axis $a$ while preserving all factual content, arguments, and structure.\n98\tWe generate both directions: $x \\rightarrow x'_{a^+}$ and $x \\rightarrow x'_{a^-}$, yielding a counterfactual pair $(x_{a^+}, x_{a^-})$ for each source.\n99\t\n100\tWe use Qwen3-32B as the primary rewriter and validate robustness with Llama-3.1-70B-Instruct (Section~\\ref{sec:rewriter})."}]},"parent_tool_use_id":"toolu_01HHJivM5WhiGQkxWgoTKbUa","session_id":"e3a9c93d-dffd-441a-83b0-1831ff4a3a69","uuid":"406b0e35-1f9b-4a32-80fb-d74fda33dd6b","timestamp":"2026-05-20T21:56:55.212Z"}
