{"type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"tool_use_id":"toolu_01PSwNM36F2Ym99xrqZXgJau","type":"tool_result","content":"100\tWe use Qwen3-32B as the primary rewriter and validate robustness with Llama-3.1-70B-Instruct (Section~\\ref{sec:rewriter}).\n101\t\n102\t\\subsection{Content Preservation Verification}\n103\t\\label{sec:verification}\n104\t\n105\tCounterfactuals must alter style without changing content.\n106\tWe enforce this with a dual-gate verification:\n107\t\\begin{itemize}\n108\t    \\item \\textbf{NLI gate}: A natural language inference model must classify the entailment between $x$ and $x'$ as entailment with score $\\geq 0.80$.\n109\t    \\item \\textbf{BERTScore gate}: BERTScore F1 between $x$ and $x'$ must exceed $0.90$.\n110\t\\end{itemize}\n111\t\n112\tOnly pairs passing \\emph{both} gates proceed to evaluation.\n113\tThis dual-gate mechanism also serves as an axis suitability filter: axes that inherently change information content (e.g., verbosity) fail verification and are correctly excluded (Section~\\ref{sec:verbosity}).\n114\t\n115\t\\subsection{Position-Controlled Pairwise Bias Measurement}\n116\t\\label{sec:measurement}\n117\t\n118\tFor each verified counterfactual pair $(x_{a^+}, x_{a^-})$, we query the LLM judge twice with swapped positions:\n119\t\\begin{align}\n120\t    r_1 &= \\text{Judge}(x_{a^+}, x_{a^-}) \\\\\n121\t    r_2 &= \\text{Judge}(x_{a^-}, x_{a^+})\n122\t\\end{align}\n123\t\n124\tA pair is \\emph{consistent} if the judge picks the same style pole in both positions.\n125\tAmong consistent pairs, we compute the win rate for each pole and test for bias using a two-tailed sign test.\n126\tWe report Wilson confidence intervals throughout.\n127\t\n128\t\\paragraph{Likert Comparison.}\n129\tFor the same counterfactual data, we also compute the average treatment effect (ATE) under Likert pointwise scoring: $\\text{ATE} = \\mathbb{E}[\\text{Score}(x_{a^+})] - \\mathbb{E}[\\text{Score}(x_{a^-})]$, tested with a paired $t$-test.\n130\t\n131\t%--------------------------------------------------------------\n132\t\\section{Experimental Setup}\n133\t\\label{sec:setup}\n134\t%--------------------------------------------------------------\n135\t\n136\t\\paragraph{Judges.} GPT-4o, Claude-3.5-Sonnet, and Gemini-2.0-Flash, representing three distinct model families and capability tiers.\n137\t\n138\t\\paragraph{Benchmarks.} LLMBar~\\cite{zeng2024llmbar} ($n{=}100$ instruction-following pairs), AlpacaEval~\\cite{dubois2024length} ($n{=}100$ open-ended instructions), and MT-Bench~\\cite{zheng2024judging} ($n{=}100$ multi-turn dialogues).\n139\t\n140\t\\paragraph{Style Axes.} Formality (formal/casual), register (academic/conversational), tone (assertive/hedging), and verbosity (verbose/concise).\n141\t\n142\t\\paragraph{Statistical Tests.} Two-tailed sign tests for pairwise win rates; paired $t$-tests for Likert ATE. We report both uncorrected and Benjamini-Hochberg corrected $p$-values where applicable. Significance: $*$ $p{<}0.05$, $**$ $p{<}0.01$, $\\dagger$ $p{<}0.10$.\n143\t\n144\t%--------------------------------------------------------------\n145\t\\section{Results}\n146\t\\label{sec:results}\n147\t%--------------------------------------------------------------\n148\t\n149\t\\subsection{Formality Bias Across Judges (LLMBar)}\n150\t\\label{sec:multijudge}\n151\t\n152\tTable~\\ref{tab:multijudge} presents formality pairwise results on LLMBar across three judges.\n153\tGPT-4o shows the strongest formality premium (69.1\\%, $p{=}0.006$), Claude-3.5-Sonnet replicates the effect at moderate magnitude (65.4\\%, $p{=}0.021$), while Gemini-2.0-Flash does not reach significance (56.3\\%, $p{=}0.136$).\n154\t\n155\tThe direction asymmetry is consistent across all judges: formal-wins are higher when the original text is formal (formal$\\rightarrow$casual direction), with the maximum gap of 24.3 percentage points on GPT-4o (78.8\\% vs.\\ 54.5\\%).\n156\tThis asymmetry reflects an additive interaction between formality preference and original-text preference, quantified in Section~\\ref{sec:original}.\n157\t\n158\tTwo of three judges yield significant results, supporting C1 with the caveat that detection magnitude is judge-dependent.\n159\tGemini-Flash's non-significant result may reflect lower sensitivity of lightweight models to subtle style variation, compounded by lower consistency (49\\% vs.\\ 54\\% for Claude).\n160\t\n161\t\\begin{table}[t]\n162\t\\centering\n163\t\\small\n164\t\\begin{tabular}{lcccccc}\n165\t\\toprule\n166\t\\textbf{Judge} & $n_c$ & \\textbf{F} & \\textbf{C} & \\textbf{WR} & \\textbf{$p$} \\\\\n167\t\\midrule\n168\tGPT-4o & 55 & 38 & 17 & 69.1\\% & .006** \\\\\n169\tClaude-3.5 & 52 & 34 & 18 & 65.4\\% & .021* \\\\\n170\tGemini-Flash & 48 & 27 & 21 & 56.3\\% & .136 \\\\\n171\t\\bottomrule\n172\t\\end{tabular}\n173\t\\caption{Multi-judge formality pairwise results on LLMBar ($n{=}100$). $n_c$: consistent pairs; F/C: formal/casual wins; WR: formal win rate.}\n174\t\\label{tab:multijudge}\n175\t\\end{table}\n176\t\n177\t\\subsection{Formality Bias Across Benchmarks}\n178\t\\label{sec:multibench}\n179\t\n180\tTable~\\ref{tab:crossbench} shows the full judge $\\times$ benchmark matrix.\n181\tA clear effect gradient emerges: LLMBar $>$ AlpacaEval $\\gg$ MT-Bench, with 4 of 9 combinations reaching significance at $p{<}0.05$.\n182\tOn AlpacaEval, both GPT-4o (62.1\\%, $p{=}0.042$) and Claude-3.5 (60.7\\%, $p{=}0.047$) detect formality bias, though Claude's result is borderline after multiple-comparison correction.\n183\tMT-Bench yields no significant results for any judge, establishing an important boundary condition: CSD's sensitivity decreases with source text complexity and multi-turn structure.\n184\t\n185\tThis benchmark gradient is interpretable: LLMBar's short, structured instruction-following pairs make formality manipulation highly salient to judges; AlpacaEval's longer, more diverse responses dilute the signal; MT-Bench's multi-turn dialogues further reduce the relative weight of surface style.\n186\t\n187\t\\begin{table}[t]\n188\t\\centering\n189\t\\small\n190\t\\begin{tabular}{lccc}\n191\t\\toprule\n192\t & \\textbf{LLMBar} & \\textbf{AlpacaEval} & \\textbf{MT-Bench} \\\\\n193\t\\midrule\n194\tGPT-4o & 69.1\\% (.006**) & 62.1\\% (.042*) & 55.8\\% (.215) \\\\\n195\tClaude-3.5 & 65.4\\% (.021*) & 60.7\\% (.047*) & 54.2\\% (.312) \\\\\n196\tGemini & 56.3\\% (.136) & 53.8\\% (.345) & 51.6\\% (.478) \\\\\n197\t\\bottomrule\n198\t\\end{tabular}\n199\t\\caption{Formality win rate (sign test $p$) across judges and benchmarks. Effect gradient: LLMBar $>$ AlpacaEval $\\gg$ MT-Bench.}\n200\t\\label{tab:crossbench}\n201\t\\end{table}\n202\t\n203\tCounterfactual quality also decreases with text complexity (Table~\\ref{tab:cfquality}): NLI pass rates are 88\\%, 85\\%, and 81\\% for LLMBar, AlpacaEval, and MT-Bench respectively, as longer and more conversational texts are harder to style-transfer without semantic drift.\n204\t\n205\t\\begin{table}[t]\n206\t\\centering\n207\t\\small\n208\t\\begin{tabular}{lcccc}\n209\t\\toprule\n210\t\\textbf{Benchmark} & \\textbf{NLI} & \\textbf{BERT} & \\textbf{Both} & \\textbf{$\\overline{\\text{NLI}}$} \\\\\n211\t\\midrule\n212\tLLMBar & 88\\% & 85\\% & 81\\% & .896 \\\\\n213\tAlpacaEval & 85\\% & 82\\% & 76\\% & .871 \\\\\n214\tMT-Bench & 81\\% & 78\\% & 69\\% & .842 \\\\\n215\t\\bottomrule\n216\t\\end{tabular}\n217\t\\caption{Counterfactual quality by benchmark. NLI/BERT: pass rates for entailment $\\geq$0.80 / BERTScore $\\geq$0.90. Both: joint pass rate.}\n218\t\\label{tab:cfquality}\n219\t\\end{table}\n220\t\n221\t\\subsection{Likert vs.\\ Pairwise Sensitivity}\n222\t\\label{sec:sensitivity}\n223\t\n224\tTable~\\ref{tab:likertvspw} presents the core evidence for C2.\n225\tAcross all four pairwise-significant judge$\\times$benchmark combinations, Likert scoring fails to reach significance.\n226\tThe gap is most striking for GPT-4o$\\times$LLMBar: pairwise yields $p{=}0.006$, while Likert gives $p{=}0.332$---and the Likert ATE direction is \\emph{wrong} ($-0.102$, indicating a casual premium contradicting the pairwise result).\n227\t\n228\tThis is not merely a sensitivity issue: Likert's absolute scoring mechanism compresses style-driven quality differences into a noise-level range ($|$ATE$|$ $<$ 0.15), and two of nine combinations show inconsistent ATE direction.\n229\tThe closest Likert approach to significance is Claude$\\times$LLMBar ($p{=}0.082$), but its ATE of $+0.138$ is still within the idiosyncratic variance of absolute scoring.\n230\t\n231\t\\begin{table}[t]\n232\t\\centering\n233\t\\small\n234\t\\setlength{\\tabcolsep}{3pt}\n235\t\\begin{tabular}{lcccccc}\n236\t\\toprule\n237\t\\textbf{J $\\times$ B} & \\textbf{L-ATE} & \\textbf{L-$p$} & \\textbf{PW} & \\textbf{P-$p$} & \\textbf{Gap} \\\\\n238\t\\midrule\n239\tG$\\times$LB & $-$0.10 & .332 & 69.1\\% & .006** & \\cmark \\\\\n240\tC$\\times$LB & $+$0.14 & .082$^\\dagger$ & 65.4\\% & .021* & \\cmark \\\\\n241\tGe$\\times$LB & $+$0.04 & .521 & 56.3\\% & .136 & --- \\\\\n242\tG$\\times$AE & $-$0.05 & .445 & 62.1\\% & .042* & \\cmark \\\\\n243\tC$\\times$AE & $+$0.07 & .258 & 60.7\\% & .047* & \\cmark \\\\\n244\tGe$\\times$AE & $+$0.02 & .674 & 53.8\\% & .345 & --- \\\\\n245\tG$\\times$MB & $-$0.03 & .612 & 55.8\\% & .215 & --- \\\\\n246\tC$\\times$MB & $+$0.05 & .398 & 54.2\\% & .312 & --- \\\\\n247\tGe$\\times$MB & $+$0.01 & .723 & 51.6\\% & .478 & --- \\\\\n248\t\\bottomrule\n249\t\\end{tabular}\n250\t\\caption{Likert vs.\\ Pairwise detection sensitivity. L-ATE: Likert average treatment effect; PW: pairwise win rate. Gap = pairwise significant but Likert not. G=GPT-4o, C=Claude, Ge=Gemini; LB=LLMBar, AE=AlpacaEval, MB=MT-Bench.}\n251\t\\label{tab:likertvspw}\n252\t\\end{table}\n253\t\n254\t\\subsection{Style Axis Spectrum}\n255\t\\label{sec:spectrum}\n256\t\n257\t\\paragraph{Register (Academic vs.\\ Conversational).}\n258\tTable~\\ref{tab:register} shows register results on LLMBar.\n259\tGPT-4o exhibits a borderline academic premium (61.4\\%, $p{=}0.048$) that is consistent with the formality finding---both axes load on a latent ``prestige'' dimension.\n260\tHowever, this $p$-value does not survive Bonferroni correction across axes ($p_{\\text{adj}}{\\approx}0.14$), and neither Claude (56.1\\%, $p{=}0.163$) nor Gemini (54.1\\%, $p{=}0.297$) reaches significance.\n261\tRegister and formality are conceptually related ($r{\\approx}0.7$ in sociolinguistic feature space), so the register signal may partly reflect the same underlying bias rather than an independent axis.\n262\t\n263\t\\begin{table}[t]\n264\t\\centering\n265\t\\small\n266\t\\begin{tabular}{lccccc}\n267\t\\toprule\n268\t\\textbf{Judge} & $n_c$ & \\textbf{Acad.} & \\textbf{Conv.} & \\textbf{WR} & \\textbf{$p$} \\\\\n269\t\\midrule\n270\tGPT-4o & 44 & 27 & 17 & 61.4\\% & .048* \\\\\n271\tClaude-3.5 & 41 & 23 & 18 & 56.1\\% & .163 \\\\\n272\tGemini-Flash & 37 & 20 & 17 & 54.1\\% & .297 \\\\\n273\t\\bottomrule\n274\t\\end{tabular}\n275\t\\caption{Register axis (academic vs.\\ conversational) pairwise results on LLMBar.}\n276\t\\label{tab:register}\n277\t\\end{table}\n278\t\n279\t\\paragraph{Tone (Assertive vs.\\ Hedging).}\n280\tTable~\\ref{tab:tone} shows tone results.\n281\tGPT-4o shows a marginal assertive premium (57.1\\%, $p{=}0.092$), but the effective sample is small ($n_c{=}42$) due to lower counterfactual quality (both-pass rate 73\\%).\n282\tPower analysis indicates that detecting an effect of $d{=}0.14$ at $n{=}42$ yields only ${\\sim}18\\%$ power; approximately $n{=}350$ consistent pairs would be needed for 80\\% power.\n283\tClaude (53.8\\%, $p{=}0.371$) and Gemini (51.4\\%, $p{=}0.500$) show no signal.\n284\t\n285\t\\begin{table}[t]\n286\t\\centering\n287\t\\small\n288\t\\begin{tabular}{lccccc}\n289\t\\toprule\n290\t\\textbf{Judge} & $n_c$ & \\textbf{Ass.} & \\textbf{Hed.} & \\textbf{WR} & \\textbf{$p$} \\\\\n291\t\\midrule\n292\tGPT-4o & 42 & 24 & 18 & 57.1\\% & .092$^\\dagger$ \\\\\n293\tClaude-3.5 & 39 & 21 & 18 & 53.8\\% & .371 \\\\\n294\tGemini-Flash & 35 & 18 & 17 & 51.4\\% & .500 \\\\\n295\t\\bottomrule\n296\t\\end{tabular}\n297\t\\caption{Tone axis (assertive vs.\\ hedging) pairwise results on LLMBar.}\n298\t\\label{tab:tone}\n299\t\\end{table}"}]},"parent_tool_use_id":"toolu_01HHJivM5WhiGQkxWgoTKbUa","session_id":"e3a9c93d-dffd-441a-83b0-1831ff4a3a69","uuid":"b5b90956-226a-499e-b414-ae5815e66785","timestamp":"2026-05-20T21:57:01.758Z"}
