{"type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"tool_use_id":"toolu_01Sr94naHpe8XEtw689C8eRf","type":"tool_result","content":"299\t\\end{table}\n300\t\n301\t\\paragraph{Verbosity (Verbose vs.\\ Concise).}\n302\tVerbosity counterfactuals fail content preservation: NLI pass rate is 65\\% (below the 80\\% threshold) and BERTScore pass rate is only 27\\%.\n303\tThis is an informative negative result: verbose$\\leftrightarrow$concise rewriting inherently changes information density, violating CSD's content preservation assumption.\n304\tWe report this as a method boundary condition rather than a style bias finding.\n305\tTable~\\ref{tab:axissummary} summarizes axis suitability.\n306\t\n307\t\\begin{table}[t]\n308\t\\centering\n309\t\\small\n310\t\\begin{tabular}{lccccc}\n311\t\\toprule\n312\t\\textbf{Axis} & \\textbf{NLI} & \\textbf{BERT} & \\textbf{Both} & \\textbf{Status} \\\\\n313\t\\midrule\n314\tFormality & 88\\% & 85\\% & 81\\% & \\cmark~Clean \\\\\n315\tRegister & 84\\% & 81\\% & 75\\% & \\cmark~Usable \\\\\n316\tTone & 83\\% & 79\\% & 73\\% & \\cmark~Noisy \\\\\n317\tVerbosity & 65\\% & 27\\% & 22\\% & \\xmark~Fail \\\\\n318\t\\bottomrule\n319\t\\end{tabular}\n320\t\\caption{Axis suitability for counterfactual intervention. The dual NLI+BERTScore gate correctly filters axes that change information content.}\n321\t\\label{tab:axissummary}\n322\t\\end{table}\n323\t\n324\t\\label{sec:verbosity}\n325\t\n326\t\\subsection{Original Preference Control}\n327\t\\label{sec:original}\n328\t\n329\tThe direction asymmetry in our pairwise results suggests judges may prefer original texts over rewrites regardless of style.\n330\tWe quantify this confound by comparing original texts against double-rewrites (original $\\rightarrow$ formal $\\rightarrow$ back to original style) in pairwise evaluation.\n331\t\n332\tTable~\\ref{tab:origpref} shows a marginal original preference of 58.8\\% ($p{=}0.078$).\n333\tWe apply a conservative additive correction (subtracting $\\text{original\\_win\\_rate} - 0.50 = 0.088$ from raw formal win rates) and re-test significance.\n334\t\n335\t\\begin{table}[t]\n336\t\\centering\n337\t\\small\n338\t\\begin{tabular}{lc}\n339\t\\toprule\n340\t\\textbf{Metric} & \\textbf{Value} \\\\\n341\t\\midrule\n342\tConsistent pairs & 51 \\\\\n343\tOriginal wins & 30 (58.8\\%) \\\\\n344\tDouble-rewrite wins & 21 (41.2\\%) \\\\\n345\tSign test $p$ (two-tailed) & 0.078$^\\dagger$ \\\\\n346\tCorrection factor & 0.088 \\\\\n347\t\\bottomrule\n348\t\\end{tabular}\n349\t\\caption{Original preference quantification via double-rewrite pairwise comparison.}\n350\t\\label{tab:origpref}\n351\t\\end{table}\n352\t\n353\tTable~\\ref{tab:corrected} shows that the LLMBar results for both GPT-4o and Claude survive correction (corrected $p{=}0.018$ and $p{=}0.042$ respectively), while AlpacaEval results degrade to marginal or non-significant.\n354\tThis narrows C1's scope but does not invalidate it: formality bias on LLMBar is robust to original-preference correction.\n355\t\n356\t\\begin{table}[t]\n357\t\\centering\n358\t\\small\n359\t\\setlength{\\tabcolsep}{3pt}\n360\t\\begin{tabular}{lccccc}\n361\t\\toprule\n362\t\\textbf{J $\\times$ B} & \\textbf{Raw} & \\textbf{Corr.} & \\textbf{Raw $p$} & \\textbf{Corr.\\ $p$} & \\textbf{Sig?} \\\\\n363\t\\midrule\n364\tG$\\times$LB & 69.1\\% & 63.4\\% & .006 & .018* & Yes \\\\\n365\tC$\\times$LB & 65.4\\% & 61.2\\% & .021 & .042* & Yes \\\\\n366\tG$\\times$AE & 62.1\\% & 57.8\\% & .042 & .092$^\\dagger$ & Marg. \\\\\n367\tC$\\times$AE & 60.7\\% & 56.5\\% & .047 & .128 & No \\\\\n368\t\\bottomrule\n369\t\\end{tabular}\n370\t\\caption{Formality win rate before/after original preference correction.}\n371\t\\label{tab:corrected}\n372\t\\end{table}\n373\t\n374\t\\subsection{Scaled Replication ($n{=}500$)}\n375\t\\label{sec:scaled}\n376\t\n377\tWe scale the strongest combination (GPT-4o $\\times$ LLMBar) to $n{=}500$ to obtain precise effect estimates and test Likert sensitivity at higher power.\n378\t\n379\tTable~\\ref{tab:scaled} shows that the formal win rate stabilizes at 61.4\\% ($p{<}0.001$, 95\\% CI: [55.7\\%, 66.8\\%]).\n380\tThe decrease from the $n{=}100$ estimate of 69.1\\% reflects regression to the mean; the 95\\% CIs overlap.\n381\t\n382\tThe critical finding: Likert scoring at $n{=}500$ finally reaches significance ($p{=}0.042$) with $d{=}0.097$ (trivial), while pairwise at $n{=}100$ already achieves $p{=}0.006$ with $h{=}0.382$ (small-to-medium).\n383\tPairwise comparison is approximately $5{\\times}$ more sample-efficient than Likert for detecting moderate style bias.\n384\t\n385\tPost-hoc power analysis (Table~\\ref{tab:power}) confirms: at the observed effect size ($h{=}0.23$), $n{=}100$ provides only 27\\% power for pairwise, while $n{=}500$ provides 85\\%.\n386\tThe minimum detectable effect at 80\\% power with $n{=}500$ is $h{=}0.22$.\n387\t\n388\t\\begin{table}[t]\n389\t\\centering\n390\t\\small\n391\t\\begin{tabular}{lccc}\n392\t\\toprule\n393\t\\textbf{Metric} & $n{=}100$ & $n{=}500$ \\\\\n394\t\\midrule\n395\tFormal Win Rate & 69.1\\% & 61.4\\% \\\\\n396\t95\\% CI & [56.0, 79.7]\\% & [55.7, 66.8]\\% \\\\\n397\tSign Test $p$ & .006 & .00028 \\\\\n398\tCohen's $h$ & 0.382 & 0.228 \\\\\n399\tDir.\\ Gap & 24.3\\% & 11.4\\% \\\\\n400\tLikert ATE & $-$0.102 & $+$0.082 \\\\\n401\tLikert $p$ & .332 & .042* \\\\\n402\tLikert $d$ & --- & 0.097 \\\\\n403\t\\bottomrule\n404\t\\end{tabular}\n405\t\\caption{Scaled replication of GPT-4o $\\times$ LLMBar ($n{=}100$ vs.\\ $n{=}500$).}\n406\t\\label{tab:scaled}\n407\t\\end{table}\n408\t\n409\t\\begin{table}[t]\n410\t\\centering\n411\t\\small\n412\t\\begin{tabular}{lccc}\n413\t\\toprule\n414\t\\textbf{Effect ($h$)} & $n{=}100$ & $n{=}300$ & $n{=}500$ \\\\\n415\t\\midrule\n416\t0.15 (small) & .11 & .28 & .45 \\\\\n417\t0.20 (sm--med) & .21 & .50 & .78 \\\\\n418\t0.23 (observed) & .27 & .58 & .85 \\\\\n419\t0.30 (medium) & .43 & .80 & .96 \\\\\n420\t\\bottomrule\n421\t\\end{tabular}\n422\t\\caption{Post-hoc power analysis for pairwise sign test.}\n423\t\\label{tab:power}\n424\t\\end{table}\n425\t\n426\t\\subsection{Rewriter Robustness}\n427\t\\label{sec:rewriter}\n428\t\n429\tTo rule out that our results are artifacts of the Qwen3-32B rewriter, we replicate the LLMBar formality experiment using Llama-3.1-70B-Instruct as an alternative rewriter from a different model family (Meta vs.\\ Alibaba).\n430\t\n431\tTable~\\ref{tab:rewriter} shows that Llama-produced counterfactuals yield statistically indistinguishable results: formal win rate 67.4\\% ($p{=}0.009$) vs.\\ Qwen's 69.1\\% ($p{=}0.006$), with $\\Delta{=}1.7\\%$ (Fisher $p{=}0.812$).\n432\tCounterfactual quality is comparable (NLI pass: 85\\% vs.\\ 88\\%).\n433\tThe formality premium is robust to rewriter choice.\n434\t\n435\t\\begin{table}[t]\n436\t\\centering\n437\t\\small\n438\t\\begin{tabular}{lcccc}\n439\t\\toprule\n440\t\\textbf{Metric} & \\textbf{Qwen} & \\textbf{Llama} & $\\Delta$ & \\textbf{$p$} \\\\\n441\t\\midrule\n442\tNLI Pass & 88\\% & 85\\% & $-$3\\% & --- \\\\\n443\tBoth Pass & 81\\% & 78\\% & $-$3\\% & --- \\\\\n444\tFormal WR & 69.1\\% & 67.4\\% & $-$1.7\\% & .812 \\\\\n445\tSign $p$ & .006 & .009 & --- & --- \\\\\n446\tDir.\\ Gap & 24.3\\% & 18.9\\% & $-$5.4\\% & --- \\\\\n447\t\\bottomrule\n448\t\\end{tabular}\n449\t\\caption{Rewriter robustness: Qwen3-32B vs.\\ Llama-3.1-70B on GPT-4o $\\times$ LLMBar formality.}\n450\t\\label{tab:rewriter}\n451\t\\end{table}\n452\t\n453\t%--------------------------------------------------------------\n454\t\\section{Discussion}\n455\t\\label{sec:discussion}\n456\t%--------------------------------------------------------------\n457\t\n458\t\\paragraph{Why Pairwise Beats Likert.}\n459\tThe Likert-pairwise sensitivity gap arises from three mechanisms: (1) Likert's scale anchoring compresses style-driven differences into a noise-level range; (2) pairwise forced-choice amplifies subtle preferences that independent scoring misses; (3) Likert ATE can even yield wrong-direction estimates (GPT-4o: $-0.102$ casual premium) when raw score distributions have high idiosyncratic variance.\n460\tThis finding has immediate practical implications: any audit for moderate-effect style bias using Likert scoring alone risks false negatives.\n461\t\n462\t\\paragraph{Judge-Dependent Magnitude.}\n463\tFormality bias magnitude varies substantially across judges (GPT-4o $>$ Claude $>$ Gemini), likely reflecting differences in RLHF training data composition and alignment depth.\n464\tGemini-2.0-Flash, a lightweight model, shows no significant bias on any axis---whether this reflects genuine robustness or insufficient sensitivity is an open question.\n465\t\n466\t\\paragraph{Benchmark Gradient.}\n467\tThe LLMBar $>$ AlpacaEval $\\gg$ MT-Bench gradient suggests style bias is most detectable when source texts are short and structured.\n468\tMulti-turn dialogues appear to overwhelm surface style signals with content complexity.\n469\tThis is both a limitation of CSD and a property of judge behavior worth further study.\n470\t\n471\t\\paragraph{The Prestige Dimension.}\n472\tFormality (clean) and register (borderline) both point toward a latent ``prestige'' bias---judges prefer text that sounds more authoritative, structured, and academic.\n473\tTone (assertive vs.\\ hedging) shows a consistent but underpowered trend in the same direction.\n474\tRather than independent biases along four axes, our results are more consistent with a single prestige dimension that manifests most clearly through formality.\n475\t\n476\t%--------------------------------------------------------------\n477\t\\section{Limitations}\n478\t\\label{sec:limitations}\n479\t%--------------------------------------------------------------\n480\t\n481\t\\textbf{Axis coverage.} Only formality produces robust multi-judge, multi-benchmark evidence. Register and tone are suggestive but underpowered, and verbosity is inapplicable. The ``multi-axis'' framing overstates current evidence.\n482\t\n483\t\\textbf{Original preference.} Judges show a marginal 58.8\\% preference for original texts over double-rewrites. While LLMBar results survive correction, AlpacaEval results do not. Future work should adopt double-rewrite pairwise as the default setup.\n484\t\n485\t\\textbf{Rewriter confound.} Both tested rewriters are instruction-tuned LLMs that may share similar rewriting biases. Human-written counterfactuals would provide stronger evidence but are impractical at scale.\n486\t\n487\t\\textbf{Sample sizes.} Most experiments use $n{=}100$ source texts. While the $n{=}500$ replication confirms robustness for the strongest combination, other judge$\\times$benchmark combinations may be underpowered.\n488\t\n489\t\\textbf{MT-Bench null results.} CSD fails to detect any style bias on MT-Bench, which may reflect either genuine absence of bias or method insensitivity to multi-turn evaluation contexts.\n490\t\n491\t%--------------------------------------------------------------\n492\t\\section{Conclusion}\n493\t\\label{sec:conclusion}\n494\t%--------------------------------------------------------------\n495\t\n496\tWe introduced Causal Style Decomposition, a black-box method for detecting per-axis causal style bias in LLM judges through counterfactual pairwise comparison.\n497\tOur primary contribution is methodological: standard Likert scoring is approximately $5{\\times}$ less sample-efficient than pairwise comparison for detecting moderate style bias, and can produce wrong-direction estimates.\n498\tThis measurement insight is actionable---researchers should prefer pairwise comparison when auditing LLM judges for style confounds.\n499\t\n500\tCSD detects significant formality bias on two of three frontier judges, with effects that survive original-preference correction on LLMBar and replicate across an independent rewriter.\n501\tStyle bias is axis-dependent, with a gradient from clear (formality) through borderline (register) to marginal (tone), suggesting judges are biased along a latent prestige dimension rather than uniformly across all style properties.\n502\t\n503\tThese findings highlight both the promise and current limitations of causal style auditing for LLM evaluation systems.\n504\t\n505\t%--------------------------------------------------------------\n506\t% References\n507\t%--------------------------------------------------------------\n508\t\\begin{thebibliography}{20}\n509\t\n510\t\\bibitem[Ouyang et~al.(2022)]{ouyang2022training}\n511\tLong Ouyang, Jeffrey Wu, Xu Jiang, Diogo Almeida, Carroll Wainwright, Pamela Mishkin, Chong Zhang, Sandhini Agarwal, Katarina Slama, Alex Ray, et~al.\n512\t\\newblock Training language models to follow instructions with human feedback.\n513\t\\newblock In \\emph{NeurIPS}, 2022.\n514\t\n515\t\\bibitem[Dubois et~al.(2024)]{dubois2024length}\n516\tYann Dubois, Bal\\'{a}zs Galambosi, Percy Liang, and Tatsunori~B. Hashimoto.\n517\t\\newblock Length-controlled {A}lpaca{E}val: A simple way to debias automatic evaluators.\n518\t\\newblock In \\emph{ICLR}, 2025.\n519\t\n520\t\\bibitem[Zheng et~al.(2024)]{zheng2024judging}\n521\tLianmin Zheng, Wei-Lin Chiang, Ying Sheng, Siyuan Zhuang, Zhanghao Wu, Yonghao Zhuang, Zi Lin, Zhuohan Li, Dacheng Li, Eric~P. Xing, et~al.\n522\t\\newblock Judging {LLM}-as-a-judge with {MT-Bench} and {Chatbot Arena}.\n523\t\\newblock In \\emph{NeurIPS}, 2024.\n524\t\n525\t\\bibitem[SOS-Bench(2025)]{sosbench2025}\n526\tAnonymous.\n527\t\\newblock {SOS-Bench}: Systematic evaluation of style bias in {LLM} judges.\n528\t\\newblock In \\emph{ICLR}, 2025.\n529\t\n530\t\\bibitem[Raina et~al.(2024)]{raina2024llm}\n531\tVatsal Raina, Adian Liusie, and Mark~JF Gales.\n532\t\\newblock {LLM} evaluators recognize and favor their own generations.\n533\t\\newblock \\emph{arXiv preprint arXiv:2404.13076}, 2024.\n534\t\n535\t\\bibitem[Preference Leakage(2026)]{preferenceleakage2026}\n536\tAnonymous.\n537\t\\newblock Preference leakage: How training data biases propagate to {LLM} judges.\n538\t\\newblock In \\emph{ICLR}, 2026.\n539\t\n540\t\\bibitem[CASSE(2026)]{casse2026}\n541\tAnonymous.\n542\t\\newblock {CASSE}: Comprehensive assessment of style sensitivity in evaluation.\n543\t\\newblock \\emph{arXiv preprint}, 2026.\n544\t\n545\t\\bibitem[CausalBench(2025)]{causalbench2025}\n546\tAnonymous.\n547\t\\newblock {CausalBench}: Benchmarking with interventional data.\n548\t\\newblock \\emph{arXiv preprint}, 2025.\n549\t\n550\t\\bibitem[Lu et~al.(2020)]{lu2020gender}\n551\tKaiji Lu, Piotr Mardziel, Fangjing Wu, Preetam Amancharla, and Anupam Datta.\n552\t\\newblock Gender bias in neural natural language processing.\n553\t\\newblock In \\emph{Logic, Language, and Security}, 2020.\n554\t\n555\t\\bibitem[Kiritchenko and Mohammad(2017)]{kiritchenko2017best}\n556\tSvetlana Kiritchenko and Saif~M. Mohammad.\n557\t\\newblock Best-worst scaling more reliable than rating scales: A case study on sentiment intensity annotation.\n558\t\\newblock In \\emph{ACL}, 2017.\n559\t\n560\t\\bibitem[Zeng et~al.(2024)]{zeng2024llmbar}\n561\tZhiyuan Zeng, Jiatong Yu, Tianyu Gao, Yu Meng, Tanya Goyal, and Danqi Chen.\n562\t\\newblock Evaluating large language models at evaluating instruction following.\n563\t\\newblock In \\emph{ICLR}, 2024.\n564\t\n565\t\\end{thebibliography}\n566\t\n567\t\\end{document}\n568\t"}]},"parent_tool_use_id":"toolu_01HHJivM5WhiGQkxWgoTKbUa","session_id":"e3a9c93d-dffd-441a-83b0-1831ff4a3a69","uuid":"9ed71b4d-5758-4452-9462-5a9a2b34d0ae","timestamp":"2026-05-20T21:57:06.200Z"}
