{"type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"tool_use_id":"toolu_01XyEJot4dvSG7bL5tvv1urF","type":"tool_result","content":[{"type":"text","text":"Now I have a thorough understanding of both the paper and its underlying experimental reality. Let me note some critical discrepancies between the paper and the actual experimental records, and compose my review.\n\n---\n\n## R1 Review: Method and Experiments\n\n### 1. Summary\n\nThis paper presents Causal Style Decomposition (CSD), a black-box post-hoc pipeline that generates binary style counterfactuals, applies NLI+BERTScore content verification, and uses position-controlled pairwise comparison to detect per-axis causal style bias in LLM judges. Experiments span 3 judges, 3 benchmarks, and 3 style axes (formality, tone, register), with the headline finding that pairwise comparison detects style biases that Likert pointwise scoring systematically misses (7/9 vs 0/9 significant cells). Robustness is supported by a scaled replication (n=500), original-preference control, and rewriter ablation.\n\n### 2. Strengths\n\n- **The Likert-Pairwise measurement gap (C2) is the paper's most valuable and novel contribution.** The systematic demonstration across 9 judge-benchmark cells that pairwise comparison detects biases invisible to Likert scoring (mean p-value 0.37 vs 0.021) has direct methodological implications for the evaluation community. No prior work has documented this gap at this scale.\n\n- **Systematic experimental coverage is well above community norms.** The 3x3x3 design (judges x benchmarks x axes) with consistent methodology across all cells addresses the standard reviewer complaint about single-setting studies. Three model families (OpenAI, Anthropic, Google) strengthens generalization claims.\n\n- **Position-controlled dual-direction pairwise comparison is methodologically sound.** Presenting each pair in both A/B and B/A order and requiring consistency to exclude position-driven preferences is a clean design for disentangling position bias from style bias.\n\n- **The verbosity failure is presented with intellectual honesty.** Rather than hiding the NLI 65% pass rate, the authors frame it as a principled method boundary (Table 7/crossaxis), demonstrating that the verification stage works as intended. This distinguishes CSD from correlational methods that would conflate style effects with content effects.\n\n- **The three-pronged robustness strategy is well-designed.** The original-preference control (53.3%, p=0.362, Table 8/origpref), scaled replication (n=500, p=3x10^-7, Table 10/scaled), and rewriter ablation (Fisher p=0.42, Table 12/rewriter) each address a distinct alternative explanation.\n\n- **Clean causal interpretation via counterfactual design.** By intervening on a single style axis while holding content constant (verified via NLI), the method supports genuine causal attribution rather than the correlational claims typical in prior work.\n\n### 3. Weaknesses\n\n#### Major\n\n- **W1. The Likert-Pairwise comparison is confounded by test choice, threatening C2.** The \"measurement gap\" (Table 4/gap) compares Likert ATE via paired t-test against pairwise win rate via sign test on consistency-filtered data. These are different statistical tests operating on different data subsets (all pairs vs. only consistent pairs) with different power profiles. The power analysis (Table power, Section 5.6.2) is somewhat circular: it shows Likert has 0.72 power at n=100 for d=0.065, but d=0.065 *is* the observed Likert effect --- the argument reduces to \"Likert is underpowered to detect its own observed effect.\" A critical missing control: applying a sign test to Likert scores (for each pair, does the formal version score strictly higher?) would test whether the gap is structural (pairwise comparison is genuinely more sensitive) or merely a test-choice artifact. Without this, C2 --- the paper's headline contribution --- rests on shaky methodological ground.\n\n- **W2. Formality and register axes likely overlap, undermining the \"decomposition\" framing.** The paper's own definitions reveal substantial overlap: formality = \"vocabulary register, sentence structure, punctuation\" and register = \"Latinate vs. Germanic vocabulary, complex vs. simple syntax.\" The discussion acknowledges formality \"spans vocabulary, syntax, and pragmatics\" while register \"spans vocabulary and syntax\" --- register is essentially a subset of formality's linguistic scope. No axis independence test is provided (e.g., correlation between formality and register counterfactual changes, or cross-axis preference overlap analysis). The effect size ordering (formality 68.4% > register 66.3%) may simply reflect that formality is a superset of register, not that they are genuinely independent dimensions. This weakens C3's claim of meaningful \"axis dependency\" and the word \"decomposition\" in the title.\n\n- **W3. No multiple comparison correction across 27+ hypothesis tests.** The paper conducts at least 27 tests (9 cells x 2 metrics for formality, plus tone, register, original-preference, scaled, rewriter) without Holm-Bonferroni, Benjamini-Hochberg, or any family-wise correction. Several borderline results are directly at risk: Claude x MT-Bench formality (p=0.065), Claude x AlpacaEval (p=0.048), Claude tone (p=0.085), GPT-4o tone (p=0.038). After correction, the \"7/9 significant\" count for formality likely drops, and the tone axis findings may lose significance entirely. This is a standard methodological requirement at top venues.\n\n- **W4. Severe numerical discrepancies between the paper and the review context raise credibility concerns.** The paper's Table 1 (formality-judges) reports Claude-Sonnet at 64.0% (n_cons=50, formal=32, casual=18, p=.028) and Gemini-Flash at 72.4% (n_cons=58, formal=42, casual=16, p=.002). The review context's Table 1 reports Claude at 65.4% (n_cons=52, formal=34, casual=18, p=.021) and Gemini at 56.3% (n_cons=48, formal=27, casual=21, p=.136). These are radically different --- Gemini goes from non-significant (56.3%, p=.136) in one version to highly significant (72.4%, p=.002) in another. The abstract claims \"Gemini-Flash consistently most susceptible\" which directly contradicts the review context where Gemini shows the weakest effect. Similarly, Table 2 (formality-matrix) differs substantially between the two versions: the paper reports MT-Bench as significant for GPT-4o (65.0%, p=.018) and Gemini (68.0%, p=.008), while the review context has both as non-significant (55.8%/p=.215 and 51.6%/p=.478). Only the GPT-4o x LLMBar cell (69.1%, p=.006) is consistent between versions. This pattern of discrepancies --- where weaker results in one version become strong in the other --- requires explanation.\n\n- **W5. High discard rate (42-52%) from consistency filtering with no selection bias analysis.** The dual-direction protocol discards all position-inconsistent pairs (e.g., 100 pairs → 48-58 consistent). This is methodologically motivated but discarding nearly half the data raises questions: are consistent pairs systematically different from inconsistent ones? Consistent pairs might be those where one version is clearly better (larger quality gap), biasing the remaining sample toward cases where the style manipulation co-occurs with perceptible quality differences. No analysis of consistent vs. inconsistent pair characteristics (NLI score, response length, task type) is provided.\n\n#### Minor\n\n- **W6. Tone and register tested only on LLMBar.** Formality benefits from a full 3x3 matrix (Table 2), but tone (Table tone) and register (Table register) are each tested on a single benchmark. This asymmetry weakens C3 --- we cannot determine whether the axis ordering (formality > register > tone) holds across benchmarks or is benchmark-specific.\n\n- **W7. No counterfactual examples.** The paper provides no actual examples of counterfactual rewrites. Readers cannot evaluate what \"formal vs. casual\" operationally means in this pipeline, or whether rewrites introduce subtle content changes that NLI might miss. Two to three annotated examples would substantially aid evaluation.\n\n- **W8. Scaled replication covers only the strongest cell.** The n=500 replication (Table scaled) is on Gemini x LLMBar (72.4% at n=100, the strongest cell in the paper's version). Replicating a weaker cell (e.g., Claude x MT-Bench) would be more informative about whether borderline results survive at scale.\n\n- **W9. Original-preference control is single-judge, single-benchmark.** Table origpref tests only GPT-4o x LLMBar. The actual experimental records reveal a significant direction asymmetry (formal→casual 78.8% vs. casual→formal 54.5%) that is not discussed in the paper. If original preference varies by judge or benchmark, the 53.3% estimate may not generalize.\n\n- **W10. Related work is compressed.** Four short paragraphs for a competitive literature. SOS-Bench, Preference Leakage, CASSE, and LC-AlpacaEval each deserve more detailed differentiation. The paper does not cite RATE (ICML 2025), which introduced the double-rewrite ATE protocol that the original-preference control mirrors.\n\n### 4. Questions for Authors\n\n1. **On W1 (critical):** If you apply a sign test to Likert scores (for each counterfactual pair, does the formal version receive a strictly higher Likert score?), does the Likert-Pairwise measurement gap persist? This would isolate the structural advantage of pairwise comparison from the test-choice confound.\n\n2. **On W4:** The paper reports Gemini-Flash as the most biased judge (72.4% on LLMBar), while the review context summary reports it as the least biased (56.3%, p=.136). Can you clarify which numbers are correct and provide the raw data?\n\n3. **On W2:** What is the Pearson or Spearman correlation between the formality counterfactual change and the register counterfactual change on the same items? If a response is rewritten for formality, how much does its register also shift?\n\n4. **On W3:** If Holm-Bonferroni correction is applied across the 9 formality pairwise tests, how many remain significant at the 0.05 level? What about across all 27+ tests?\n\n5. **On W5:** Can you report mean NLI score, mean response length, and task-type distribution for consistent vs. inconsistent pairs? Is there a systematic pattern in what gets discarded?\n\n6. **On W9:** The experimental records show a direction asymmetry of 78.8% (formal→casual) vs. 54.5% (casual→formal). Why is this not discussed in the paper? Does this asymmetry replicate across judges and benchmarks?\n\n7. What is the total computational cost (API calls, USD, wall time) for a single 100-pair cell of the CSD pipeline?\n\n### 5. Minor Issues\n\n- The BERTScore threshold in Stage 2 is not specified --- the NLI threshold is 0.85, but the paper only says BERTScore is a \"secondary semantic similarity check\" without defining the pass criterion. The experimental records suggest BERTScore was eventually dropped as a gating criterion (D003, D013) but the paper still lists it as part of the method.\n- Table caption for Table 1 (formality-judges) says \"All three judges show significant formality premium,\" but this is only true in the paper's version of the numbers, not in the review context version.\n- The abstract claims \"mean win rate 66.3%\" for formality but Table 2 (formality-matrix) shows a mean of 66.3%, which appears to be the cross-axis mean, not the formality-specific mean (which is 68.4% per Table crossaxis). Clarify which average is being reported.\n- Power analysis table (Table power) reports power for d=0.065, but the observed Likert ATE in Table gap is +0.10, not 0.065. The inconsistency between these values needs explanation.\n\n### 6. Overall Assessment\n\n**Score: 3.5/6 (Borderline Conference)**\n\nThe paper addresses a timely problem (style bias in LLM judges) with a well-designed counterfactual methodology. The Likert-Pairwise measurement gap (C2) is the strongest contribution --- if it survives methodological scrutiny (same-test comparison, W1), it has genuine implications for evaluation methodology. The systematic 3x3x3 experimental coverage exceeds community norms for this type of study.\n\nHowever, several issues prevent a higher score. The C2 headline is at risk from the test-choice confound (W1). The axis independence needed for the \"decomposition\" framing is untested (W2). Multiple comparison correction is absent (W3). The numerical discrepancies between the paper and the review context are concerning (W4), and the high discard rate lacks selection bias analysis (W5). The CSD framework itself is an incremental combination of known components (counterfactual generation, NLI verification, pairwise comparison, sign test); the novelty lies in the specific combination and the empirical findings, not in methodological innovation.\n\nThe paper is above the threshold for Findings-level acceptance (sound methodology, systematic experiments, useful practical finding) but needs the W1 control (sign test on Likert) and W3 correction (multiple comparisons) to be competitive for the main conference. If the measurement gap survives same-test comparison, this becomes a solid 4.0; if it does not, the paper drops to a competent but incremental framework study at 3.0.\n\n### 7. Confidence\n\n**4/5 (High).** I am familiar with the LLM-as-judge evaluation literature, counterfactual methods in NLP, and the statistical considerations involved. My main uncertainty is about whether the test-choice confound (W1) fully explains the measurement gap or whether a structural advantage of pairwise comparison remains after controlling for it."},{"type":"text","text":"agentId: a51498beb2448a11b (use SendMessage with to: 'a51498beb2448a11b' to continue this agent)\n<usage>total_tokens: 50352\ntool_uses: 13\nduration_ms: 144891</usage>"}]}]},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"e3a9c93d-dffd-441a-83b0-1831ff4a3a69","uuid":"d72408a6-560f-454d-8094-3ab39525c29f","timestamp":"2026-05-20T21:58:17.545Z","tool_use_result":{"status":"completed","prompt":"You are Reviewer 1 (R1) for a top ML venue review. Your focus areas are **Method** and **Experiments**. Review the paper below independently and produce a structured review. Use a 6-point scale: 1=Strong Reject, 2=Reject, 3=Weak Reject, 4=Weak Accept, 5=Accept, 6=Strong Accept. Target venue acceptance rate ~25%.\n\n## Paper Summary\n\n**Title**: Causal Style Decomposition: Revealing Axis-Dependent Style Bias in LLM Judges via Counterfactual Pairwise Comparison\n\nThe paper proposes CSD (Causal Style Decomposition), a black-box post-hoc method to detect per-axis causal style bias in LLM judges. It generates binary style counterfactuals (text pairs differing only along one style axis, e.g., formal vs. casual), verifies content preservation via NLI+BERTScore dual gate, and measures bias via position-controlled pairwise comparison. \n\nThree claims:\n- C1 (Detection): Formality bias is significant on 2/3 frontier judges (GPT-4o 69.1% p=0.006, Claude 65.4% p=0.021), judge-dependent in magnitude. Replicates with independent rewriter (Llama-3.1-70B, 67.4% p=0.009). Survives original-preference correction on LLMBar.\n- C2 (Measurement): Likert scoring fails at n=100 (p=0.332) while pairwise is significant (p=0.006). At n=500, Likert barely reaches p=0.042 (d=0.097) while pairwise gives p<0.001 (h=0.228). Pairwise is ~5x more sample-efficient.\n- C3 (Axis spectrum): formality=clean, register=borderline (p=0.048), tone=marginal (p=0.092), verbosity=fail (NLI 65%). Gradient tracks \"prestige\" dimension.\n\n## Key Experimental Results\n\n### Table 1: Multi-judge formality pairwise (LLMBar, n=100)\n| Judge | n_consistent | Formal Wins | Casual Wins | Win Rate | p |\n|-------|-------------|-------------|-------------|----------|------|\n| GPT-4o | 55 | 38 | 17 | 69.1% | .006** |\n| Claude-3.5 | 52 | 34 | 18 | 65.4% | .021* |\n| Gemini-Flash | 48 | 27 | 21 | 56.3% | .136 |\n\n### Table 2: Formality win rate across judges × benchmarks\n|  | LLMBar | AlpacaEval | MT-Bench |\n|--|--------|------------|----------|\n| GPT-4o | 69.1% (.006**) | 62.1% (.042*) | 55.8% (.215) |\n| Claude-3.5 | 65.4% (.021*) | 60.7% (.047*) | 54.2% (.312) |\n| Gemini | 56.3% (.136) | 53.8% (.345) | 51.6% (.478) |\n\n### Table 4: Likert vs Pairwise sensitivity (all 9 combos)\nAll 4 pairwise-significant combos have Likert NS. GPT-4o×LLMBar: Likert ATE = -0.102 (wrong direction!), p=0.332 vs pairwise p=0.006.\n\n### Table 7: Axis suitability\n| Axis | NLI | BERT | Both | Status |\n|------|-----|------|------|--------|\n| Formality | 88% | 85% | 81% | Clean |\n| Register | 84% | 81% | 75% | Usable |\n| Tone | 83% | 79% | 73% | Noisy |\n| Verbosity | 65% | 27% | 22% | Fail |\n\n### Table 8: Original preference control\nOriginal wins 58.8% (p=0.078†). Correction factor = 0.088. After correction: GPT-4o×LLMBar still significant (63.4%, p=0.018*), Claude×LLMBar still significant (61.2%, p=0.042*). AlpacaEval degrades to marginal/NS.\n\n### Table 10: Scaled replication (n=500 GPT-4o×LLMBar)\nWin rate drops from 69.1% to 61.4% (regression to mean), p<0.001. Likert at n=500: p=0.042, d=0.097. Direction gap narrows from 24.3% to 11.4%.\n\n### Table 12: Rewriter robustness\nLlama-3.1-70B: 67.4% (p=0.009) vs Qwen 69.1% (p=0.006). Fisher p=0.812 — no difference.\n\n## Experiment Design Reference (what was planned)\n9 experiments were planned (exp-001 through exp-009). The paper covers all of them:\n- exp-001: Multi-judge formality on LLMBar ✓\n- exp-002: Multi-benchmark formality ✓\n- exp-003: Likert vs Pairwise sensitivity ✓\n- exp-004: Tone axis ✓\n- exp-005: Original preference control ✓\n- exp-006: Scaled replication n=500 ✓\n- exp-007: Verbosity boundary ✓\n- exp-008: Register axis ✓\n- exp-009: Rewriter robustness ✓\n\n## Your Review Task\n\nProduce a structured review covering:\n1. **Summary** (2-3 sentences)\n2. **Strengths** (bulleted, focus on method soundness and experimental rigor)\n3. **Weaknesses** (bulleted, focus on methodological issues, statistical concerns, missing controls, experimental gaps)\n4. **Questions for Authors** (specific, actionable)\n5. **Minor Issues**\n6. **Overall Assessment** (score 1-6 with justification)\n7. **Confidence** (1-5)\n\nBe rigorous and specific. Reference table numbers and specific numbers from the results.","agentId":"a51498beb2448a11b","agentType":"general-purpose","content":[{"type":"text","text":"Now I have a thorough understanding of both the paper and its underlying experimental reality. Let me note some critical discrepancies between the paper and the actual experimental records, and compose my review.\n\n---\n\n## R1 Review: Method and Experiments\n\n### 1. Summary\n\nThis paper presents Causal Style Decomposition (CSD), a black-box post-hoc pipeline that generates binary style counterfactuals, applies NLI+BERTScore content verification, and uses position-controlled pairwise comparison to detect per-axis causal style bias in LLM judges. Experiments span 3 judges, 3 benchmarks, and 3 style axes (formality, tone, register), with the headline finding that pairwise comparison detects style biases that Likert pointwise scoring systematically misses (7/9 vs 0/9 significant cells). Robustness is supported by a scaled replication (n=500), original-preference control, and rewriter ablation.\n\n### 2. Strengths\n\n- **The Likert-Pairwise measurement gap (C2) is the paper's most valuable and novel contribution.** The systematic demonstration across 9 judge-benchmark cells that pairwise comparison detects biases invisible to Likert scoring (mean p-value 0.37 vs 0.021) has direct methodological implications for the evaluation community. No prior work has documented this gap at this scale.\n\n- **Systematic experimental coverage is well above community norms.** The 3x3x3 design (judges x benchmarks x axes) with consistent methodology across all cells addresses the standard reviewer complaint about single-setting studies. Three model families (OpenAI, Anthropic, Google) strengthens generalization claims.\n\n- **Position-controlled dual-direction pairwise comparison is methodologically sound.** Presenting each pair in both A/B and B/A order and requiring consistency to exclude position-driven preferences is a clean design for disentangling position bias from style bias.\n\n- **The verbosity failure is presented with intellectual honesty.** Rather than hiding the NLI 65% pass rate, the authors frame it as a principled method boundary (Table 7/crossaxis), demonstrating that the verification stage works as intended. This distinguishes CSD from correlational methods that would conflate style effects with content effects.\n\n- **The three-pronged robustness strategy is well-designed.** The original-preference control (53.3%, p=0.362, Table 8/origpref), scaled replication (n=500, p=3x10^-7, Table 10/scaled), and rewriter ablation (Fisher p=0.42, Table 12/rewriter) each address a distinct alternative explanation.\n\n- **Clean causal interpretation via counterfactual design.** By intervening on a single style axis while holding content constant (verified via NLI), the method supports genuine causal attribution rather than the correlational claims typical in prior work.\n\n### 3. Weaknesses\n\n#### Major\n\n- **W1. The Likert-Pairwise comparison is confounded by test choice, threatening C2.** The \"measurement gap\" (Table 4/gap) compares Likert ATE via paired t-test against pairwise win rate via sign test on consistency-filtered data. These are different statistical tests operating on different data subsets (all pairs vs. only consistent pairs) with different power profiles. The power analysis (Table power, Section 5.6.2) is somewhat circular: it shows Likert has 0.72 power at n=100 for d=0.065, but d=0.065 *is* the observed Likert effect --- the argument reduces to \"Likert is underpowered to detect its own observed effect.\" A critical missing control: applying a sign test to Likert scores (for each pair, does the formal version score strictly higher?) would test whether the gap is structural (pairwise comparison is genuinely more sensitive) or merely a test-choice artifact. Without this, C2 --- the paper's headline contribution --- rests on shaky methodological ground.\n\n- **W2. Formality and register axes likely overlap, undermining the \"decomposition\" framing.** The paper's own definitions reveal substantial overlap: formality = \"vocabulary register, sentence structure, punctuation\" and register = \"Latinate vs. Germanic vocabulary, complex vs. simple syntax.\" The discussion acknowledges formality \"spans vocabulary, syntax, and pragmatics\" while register \"spans vocabulary and syntax\" --- register is essentially a subset of formality's linguistic scope. No axis independence test is provided (e.g., correlation between formality and register counterfactual changes, or cross-axis preference overlap analysis). The effect size ordering (formality 68.4% > register 66.3%) may simply reflect that formality is a superset of register, not that they are genuinely independent dimensions. This weakens C3's claim of meaningful \"axis dependency\" and the word \"decomposition\" in the title.\n\n- **W3. No multiple comparison correction across 27+ hypothesis tests.** The paper conducts at least 27 tests (9 cells x 2 metrics for formality, plus tone, register, original-preference, scaled, rewriter) without Holm-Bonferroni, Benjamini-Hochberg, or any family-wise correction. Several borderline results are directly at risk: Claude x MT-Bench formality (p=0.065), Claude x AlpacaEval (p=0.048), Claude tone (p=0.085), GPT-4o tone (p=0.038). After correction, the \"7/9 significant\" count for formality likely drops, and the tone axis findings may lose significance entirely. This is a standard methodological requirement at top venues.\n\n- **W4. Severe numerical discrepancies between the paper and the review context raise credibility concerns.** The paper's Table 1 (formality-judges) reports Claude-Sonnet at 64.0% (n_cons=50, formal=32, casual=18, p=.028) and Gemini-Flash at 72.4% (n_cons=58, formal=42, casual=16, p=.002). The review context's Table 1 reports Claude at 65.4% (n_cons=52, formal=34, casual=18, p=.021) and Gemini at 56.3% (n_cons=48, formal=27, casual=21, p=.136). These are radically different --- Gemini goes from non-significant (56.3%, p=.136) in one version to highly significant (72.4%, p=.002) in another. The abstract claims \"Gemini-Flash consistently most susceptible\" which directly contradicts the review context where Gemini shows the weakest effect. Similarly, Table 2 (formality-matrix) differs substantially between the two versions: the paper reports MT-Bench as significant for GPT-4o (65.0%, p=.018) and Gemini (68.0%, p=.008), while the review context has both as non-significant (55.8%/p=.215 and 51.6%/p=.478). Only the GPT-4o x LLMBar cell (69.1%, p=.006) is consistent between versions. This pattern of discrepancies --- where weaker results in one version become strong in the other --- requires explanation.\n\n- **W5. High discard rate (42-52%) from consistency filtering with no selection bias analysis.** The dual-direction protocol discards all position-inconsistent pairs (e.g., 100 pairs → 48-58 consistent). This is methodologically motivated but discarding nearly half the data raises questions: are consistent pairs systematically different from inconsistent ones? Consistent pairs might be those where one version is clearly better (larger quality gap), biasing the remaining sample toward cases where the style manipulation co-occurs with perceptible quality differences. No analysis of consistent vs. inconsistent pair characteristics (NLI score, response length, task type) is provided.\n\n#### Minor\n\n- **W6. Tone and register tested only on LLMBar.** Formality benefits from a full 3x3 matrix (Table 2), but tone (Table tone) and register (Table register) are each tested on a single benchmark. This asymmetry weakens C3 --- we cannot determine whether the axis ordering (formality > register > tone) holds across benchmarks or is benchmark-specific.\n\n- **W7. No counterfactual examples.** The paper provides no actual examples of counterfactual rewrites. Readers cannot evaluate what \"formal vs. casual\" operationally means in this pipeline, or whether rewrites introduce subtle content changes that NLI might miss. Two to three annotated examples would substantially aid evaluation.\n\n- **W8. Scaled replication covers only the strongest cell.** The n=500 replication (Table scaled) is on Gemini x LLMBar (72.4% at n=100, the strongest cell in the paper's version). Replicating a weaker cell (e.g., Claude x MT-Bench) would be more informative about whether borderline results survive at scale.\n\n- **W9. Original-preference control is single-judge, single-benchmark.** Table origpref tests only GPT-4o x LLMBar. The actual experimental records reveal a significant direction asymmetry (formal→casual 78.8% vs. casual→formal 54.5%) that is not discussed in the paper. If original preference varies by judge or benchmark, the 53.3% estimate may not generalize.\n\n- **W10. Related work is compressed.** Four short paragraphs for a competitive literature. SOS-Bench, Preference Leakage, CASSE, and LC-AlpacaEval each deserve more detailed differentiation. The paper does not cite RATE (ICML 2025), which introduced the double-rewrite ATE protocol that the original-preference control mirrors.\n\n### 4. Questions for Authors\n\n1. **On W1 (critical):** If you apply a sign test to Likert scores (for each counterfactual pair, does the formal version receive a strictly higher Likert score?), does the Likert-Pairwise measurement gap persist? This would isolate the structural advantage of pairwise comparison from the test-choice confound.\n\n2. **On W4:** The paper reports Gemini-Flash as the most biased judge (72.4% on LLMBar), while the review context summary reports it as the least biased (56.3%, p=.136). Can you clarify which numbers are correct and provide the raw data?\n\n3. **On W2:** What is the Pearson or Spearman correlation between the formality counterfactual change and the register counterfactual change on the same items? If a response is rewritten for formality, how much does its register also shift?\n\n4. **On W3:** If Holm-Bonferroni correction is applied across the 9 formality pairwise tests, how many remain significant at the 0.05 level? What about across all 27+ tests?\n\n5. **On W5:** Can you report mean NLI score, mean response length, and task-type distribution for consistent vs. inconsistent pairs? Is there a systematic pattern in what gets discarded?\n\n6. **On W9:** The experimental records show a direction asymmetry of 78.8% (formal→casual) vs. 54.5% (casual→formal). Why is this not discussed in the paper? Does this asymmetry replicate across judges and benchmarks?\n\n7. What is the total computational cost (API calls, USD, wall time) for a single 100-pair cell of the CSD pipeline?\n\n### 5. Minor Issues\n\n- The BERTScore threshold in Stage 2 is not specified --- the NLI threshold is 0.85, but the paper only says BERTScore is a \"secondary semantic similarity check\" without defining the pass criterion. The experimental records suggest BERTScore was eventually dropped as a gating criterion (D003, D013) but the paper still lists it as part of the method.\n- Table caption for Table 1 (formality-judges) says \"All three judges show significant formality premium,\" but this is only true in the paper's version of the numbers, not in the review context version.\n- The abstract claims \"mean win rate 66.3%\" for formality but Table 2 (formality-matrix) shows a mean of 66.3%, which appears to be the cross-axis mean, not the formality-specific mean (which is 68.4% per Table crossaxis). Clarify which average is being reported.\n- Power analysis table (Table power) reports power for d=0.065, but the observed Likert ATE in Table gap is +0.10, not 0.065. The inconsistency between these values needs explanation.\n\n### 6. Overall Assessment\n\n**Score: 3.5/6 (Borderline Conference)**\n\nThe paper addresses a timely problem (style bias in LLM judges) with a well-designed counterfactual methodology. The Likert-Pairwise measurement gap (C2) is the strongest contribution --- if it survives methodological scrutiny (same-test comparison, W1), it has genuine implications for evaluation methodology. The systematic 3x3x3 experimental coverage exceeds community norms for this type of study.\n\nHowever, several issues prevent a higher score. The C2 headline is at risk from the test-choice confound (W1). The axis independence needed for the \"decomposition\" framing is untested (W2). Multiple comparison correction is absent (W3). The numerical discrepancies between the paper and the review context are concerning (W4), and the high discard rate lacks selection bias analysis (W5). The CSD framework itself is an incremental combination of known components (counterfactual generation, NLI verification, pairwise comparison, sign test); the novelty lies in the specific combination and the empirical findings, not in methodological innovation.\n\nThe paper is above the threshold for Findings-level acceptance (sound methodology, systematic experiments, useful practical finding) but needs the W1 control (sign test on Likert) and W3 correction (multiple comparisons) to be competitive for the main conference. If the measurement gap survives same-test comparison, this becomes a solid 4.0; if it does not, the paper drops to a competent but incremental framework study at 3.0.\n\n### 7. Confidence\n\n**4/5 (High).** I am familiar with the LLM-as-judge evaluation literature, counterfactual methods in NLP, and the statistical considerations involved. My main uncertainty is about whether the test-choice confound (W1) fully explains the measurement gap or whether a structural advantage of pairwise comparison remains after controlling for it."}],"totalDurationMs":144891,"totalTokens":50352,"totalToolUseCount":13,"usage":{"input_tokens":1,"cache_creation_input_tokens":4601,"cache_read_input_tokens":42347,"output_tokens":3403,"server_tool_use":{"web_search_requests":0,"web_fetch_requests":0},"service_tier":"standard","cache_creation":{"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":0,"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":4601},"inference_geo":"","iterations":[{"input_tokens":1,"output_tokens":3403,"cache_read_input_tokens":42347,"cache_creation_input_tokens":4601,"cache_creation":{"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":4601,"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":0},"type":"message"}],"speed":"standard"},"toolStats":{"readCount":5,"searchCount":2,"bashCount":0,"editFileCount":0,"linesAdded":0,"linesRemoved":0,"otherToolCount":6}}}
{"type":"system","subtype":"task_progress","task_id":"aa1f51bb281c12b77","tool_use_id":"toolu_01HHJivM5WhiGQkxWgoTKbUa","description":"R2: Novelty + Related Work review","usage":{"total_tokens":84979,"tool_uses":20,"duration_ms":158382},"last_tool_name":"mcp__agent-ml-research__submit_dream_review_report","uuid":"165a1766-9bf7-4a66-85cc-0fa8bacacd04","session_id":"e3a9c93d-dffd-441a-83b0-1831ff4a3a69"}
{"type":"assistant","message":{"model":"claude-opus-4-6","id":"msg_01XfF88sxFaa1kCSiYceCYcw","type":"message","role":"assistant","content":[{"type":"tool_use","id":"toolu_015BSLun8GgszATu4szgVt3t","name":"mcp__agent-ml-research__submit_dream_review_report","input":{"report":"\n# Reviewer 2 (R2) — Novelty/Contribution & Related Work/Positioning\n\n## Focus Areas: Novelty, Contribution Significance, Related Work Coverage, Positioning\n\n---\n\n## 1. Summary\n\nThis paper introduces Causal Style Decomposition (CSD), a black-box post-hoc method for detecting per-axis causal style bias in LLM judges. CSD generates binary counterfactual text pairs along a single style axis (e.g., formal vs. casual), verifies content preservation via NLI and BERTScore, and measures bias through position-controlled pairwise comparison. The paper reports three findings: (1) formality bias is detectable across multiple judges, (2) pairwise comparison is substantially more sensitive than Likert scoring for detecting style bias, and (3) bias magnitude varies across style axes and judges.\n\n---\n\n## 2. Strengths\n\n- **The Likert-vs-pairwise measurement gap (C2) is the strongest contribution.** The systematic demonstration that Likert scoring fails to detect biases that pairwise comparison reveals (7/9 combinations in the best version, 4/4 pairwise-significant combos in the median version) is a genuinely useful methodological insight. The n=500 scaled replication showing Likert needs ~5x the sample size is actionable for the community. This finding alone justifies the paper's existence.\n\n- **Clean experimental design for the formality axis.** The pipeline (counterfactual generation, dual-gate content verification, position-controlled pairwise comparison, sign test) is methodologically sound. Position control eliminates a known confound. The dual-gate verification is a sensible quality filter.\n\n- **Appropriate robustness checks.** The original-preference control via double-rewrite, rewriter ablation (Qwen vs. Llama), and scaled replication (n=500) address the most obvious confounds. The verbosity failure as a negative control / method boundary is well-framed.\n\n- **Honest reporting in the median version.** The median paper version is notably more honest than the best version about the actual evidence (2/3 judges significant, 4/9 combos significant, register borderline, tone marginal). This transparency would serve the paper well.\n\n---\n\n## 3. Weaknesses\n\n### 3.1 Novelty Concerns (Major)\n\n- **The counterfactual-rewrite-then-measure pipeline is not novel.** The literature map explicitly lists \"Counterfactual LLM-based Framework for Measuring Rhetorical Style\" (2025, arXiv:2512.19908), which uses an essentially identical pipeline: LLM counterfactual rewrite + statistical aggregation. RATE (ICML 2025) provides the double-rewrite protocol that CSD adopts. CSD's novelty reduces to applying an existing technique to a specific problem (style bias in LLM judges) with pairwise instead of pointwise measurement. This is a valid contribution but should not be oversold as a \"framework.\"\n\n- **The pairwise-vs-Likert sensitivity difference is known in psychometrics.** Best-worst scaling and pairwise comparison have been known to be more sensitive than Likert for decades (Kiritchenko & Mohammad 2017, which the paper cites). The paper's contribution is demonstrating this in the specific context of LLM judge style bias detection, which is useful but incremental. The paper should acknowledge this prior knowledge explicitly rather than framing it as a \"striking finding.\"\n\n- **Per-axis decomposition is conceptually straightforward.** Testing one axis at a time by generating axis-specific counterfactuals is not a methodological innovation — it is a natural application of the single-variable intervention principle from causal inference. The \"decomposition\" framing suggests a more sophisticated method than what is delivered.\n\n### 3.2 Overclaiming (Major)\n\n- **Critical discrepancy between \"best\" and \"median\" paper versions.** The best version claims 7/9 significant judge x benchmark combinations, 3/3 judges significant on formality, all three non-formality axes working cleanly (formality 68.4% > register 66.3% > tone 63.4%, all 3/3 judges significant for register). The median version — which appears to reflect the actual experimental data more faithfully — reports only 4/9 significant, 2/3 judges significant, register borderline (GPT-4o only, p=0.048, does not survive multiple comparison correction), and tone marginal (GPT-4o only, p=0.092). **The best version fabricates results that the actual data does not support.** Specifically:\n  - The best version shows Gemini-Flash as \"consistently most susceptible\" (72.4% on LLMBar), but the median data shows Gemini at 56.3% (p=0.136, NOT significant).\n  - The best version shows register at 3/3 judges significant with 66.3% mean WR. The actual data shows 1/3 significant (GPT-4o borderline p=0.048).\n  - The best version shows tone at 2/3 judges significant. The actual data shows 0/3 significant (GPT-4o marginal p=0.092).\n\n  This is the most serious issue. The \"best\" paper presents projected/aspirational results as if they were collected data.\n\n- **C3 (axis spectrum) overstates the evidence.** Even in the median version, the \"spectrum\" finding reduces to: formality works, register is borderline on one judge, tone is marginal on one judge, verbosity fails. This is closer to \"formality is the only axis where CSD reliably works\" than \"style bias is axis-dependent with a systematic gradient.\" The \"prestige dimension\" interpretation is post-hoc speculation without supporting evidence (no factor analysis, no correlation with sociolinguistic prestige scales).\n\n### 3.3 Related Work Gaps (Moderate)\n\n- **Only 10-11 references is thin for a venue like EMNLP.** Several important related works are missing:\n  - **Soumik et al. (2026), \"Judging the Judges\"**: Listed in the project's own literature_map.yaml as a direct competitor (tests 9 debiasing strategies, reports bias scores 0.76-0.92) but not cited in the paper. This is a significant omission since it validates the premise and provides a natural comparison point.\n  - **FairJudge (Wang et al. 2026)**: Also in the literature map as \"most directly relevant concurrent work\" — trains a debiased judge. Should be discussed to clarify CSD's positioning as detection-only vs. correction.\n  - **RATE (Shen et al., ICML 2025)**: The paper's decisions.yaml reveals CSD directly adopts the RATE double-rewrite protocol, yet RATE is not cited in either paper version. This is a critical attribution gap.\n  - **Veitch et al. (UAI 2020)**: In the literature map as providing \"functional intervention framework for binary text treatments\" — the theoretical foundation for CSD's approach. Not cited.\n  - **Chernozhukov et al. (2018) DML**: In the literature map but not relevant to the final method (CSD does not use DML). Correctly omitted.\n  \n- **No discussion of \"Counterfactual Rhetorical\" (2025)**, which uses the same pipeline structure. This is the closest methodological competitor and its omission weakens the novelty claim.\n\n### 3.4 Contribution Significance (Moderate)\n\n- **CSD is detection-only.** The paper detects style bias but proposes no correction or mitigation. While detection is valuable, the practical impact is limited — a practitioner who discovers formality bias using CSD still has no CSD-derived method to fix it. LC-AlpacaEval, by contrast, provides both detection and correction.\n\n- **The scope of validated findings is narrow.** Robust evidence exists for one axis (formality) on two judges (GPT-4o, Claude) on two benchmarks (LLMBar, AlpacaEval) — and even AlpacaEval results do not survive original-preference correction. This is a useful pilot finding but thin for a main conference paper at a venue with ~25% acceptance.\n\n- **No comparison to existing debiasing baselines.** The paper does not compare CSD's bias detection sensitivity against LC-AlpacaEval's regression approach or any other method. Without such comparison, it is unclear whether CSD's counterfactual approach provides better detection than simpler alternatives.\n\n---\n\n## 4. Questions for Authors\n\n1. **Data integrity**: The \"best\" paper version reports 7/9 significant formality combinations and 3/3 judges significant on all axes, while the actual experimental data (median version) shows 4/9 and 2/3. Which numbers will appear in the submitted paper, and are the \"best\" version numbers projections or actual results?\n\n2. **RATE attribution**: The decisions.yaml shows CSD directly adopts RATE's double-rewrite protocol. Why is RATE not cited? How does CSD differ from running RATE with style-specific counterfactuals?\n\n3. **Register-formality correlation**: You acknowledge register and formality are \"conceptually related (r~0.7 in sociolinguistic feature space).\" If they load on the same factor, what does the \"per-axis decomposition\" actually decompose? Have you tested whether register results survive partialing out formality?\n\n4. **Likert implementation**: What Likert scale did you use (5-point? 10-point?)? The decisions reveal \"64% zero differences\" with integer Likert at n=100, suggesting the scale granularity is part of the problem. Did you try continuous or logprob-based scoring as an alternative pointwise method? The gap might be Likert-specific rather than pointwise-specific.\n\n5. **Why no Bonferroni/BH correction in the best version?** The best version reports 7/9 significant without any multiple comparison correction. The median version correctly notes that register p=0.048 does not survive Bonferroni. This should be standard practice.\n\n6. **Original preference correction scope**: You ran the original-preference control only on GPT-4o x LLMBar. Do you have evidence that the 53.3% / 58.8% original preference generalizes across judges and benchmarks? If Gemini has different original preference, the correction may not transfer.\n\n---\n\n## 5. Minor Issues\n\n- The paper title in the best version (\"Causal Style Decomposition Reveals Systematic Style Bias in LLM-as-Judge That Likert Scoring Misses\") is stronger but potentially overclaims. The median version title is more accurate.\n- BERTScore threshold inconsistency: Abstract/method says >=0.90, but the decisions.yaml reveals BERTScore was demoted to monitoring-only (D003) because it penalizes legitimate style transfer. The paper should either use BERTScore consistently or explain why it was relaxed.\n- The \"four-stage pipeline\" claim in the best version method section lists 4 stages, but the median version correctly describes 3 stages. The statistical analysis is not a separate pipeline stage.\n- Several bibliography entries use \"Anonymous\" as author — these need to be filled in for camera-ready.\n- No error bars on the cross-axis summary table (Table 7 in best version). Wilson CIs should be reported for all win rates.\n\n---\n\n## 6. Overall Assessment\n\n**Score: 3.0 (Findings)**\n\n**Justification:**\n\nThe paper's strongest contribution — the Likert-vs-pairwise measurement gap — is a genuinely useful methodological insight for the LLM evaluation community. The formality bias detection results on LLMBar are credible and survive robustness checks. However, the paper faces several issues that prevent it from reaching the main conference bar:\n\n(1) **Novelty is incremental.** The counterfactual-rewrite pipeline exists in prior work (Counterfactual Rhetorical 2025, RATE 2025). The pairwise > Likert sensitivity is known from psychometrics. CSD's contribution is combining these known ideas in a new application context — useful but not a strong novelty claim.\n\n(2) **The actual evidence is narrower than presented.** Only formality produces robust multi-judge results. Register and tone are borderline/marginal on one judge only. The \"axis spectrum\" and \"prestige dimension\" framing oversells what the data supports.\n\n(3) **Missing critical related work and attributions.** RATE, Counterfactual Rhetorical, Soumik 2026, and FairJudge 2026 all need discussion. The 10-reference bibliography is insufficient.\n\n(4) **Detection without correction limits practical impact.** The paper tells practitioners \"you have a bias\" but not what to do about it.\n\n(5) **The discrepancy between best and median versions raises concerns about result reliability.** If the submitted paper uses the best version numbers, several results appear to be projections rather than actual experimental outcomes.\n\nThe paper is suitable for Findings if: (a) it uses the median version's honest numbers, (b) adds missing related work and RATE attribution, (c) reframes C3 as preliminary/suggestive rather than established, and (d) foregrounds C2 (measurement gap) as the primary contribution. For Main, the paper would need additional working axes with robust multi-judge evidence and a comparison against existing detection methods.\n\n---\n\n## 7. Confidence\n\n**4/5 (High)**\n\nI have deep familiarity with the LLM-as-judge evaluation literature, counterfactual methods in NLP, and the specific papers in this space (SOS-Bench, LC-AlpacaEval, CASSE, Preference Leakage). I reviewed the full paper, experimental data, and development history carefully. My main uncertainty is whether the \"best\" version numbers represent planned experiments that will be completed before submission — if so, the final paper may be stronger than what I reviewed. However, I reviewed the evidence as presented.\n","project":"causal_style_decomp_judge","tier":"best","dream_id":"dream_20260520T211832","overall_score":"3.0 - Findings"},"caller":{"type":"direct"}}],"stop_reason":null,"stop_sequence":null,"stop_details":null,"usage":{"input_tokens":3,"cache_creation_input_tokens":765,"cache_read_input_tokens":83925,"cache_creation":{"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":765,"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":0},"output_tokens":2,"service_tier":"standard","inference_geo":"not_available"},"diagnostics":null,"context_management":null},"parent_tool_use_id":"toolu_01HHJivM5WhiGQkxWgoTKbUa","session_id":"e3a9c93d-dffd-441a-83b0-1831ff4a3a69","uuid":"d4459803-4e79-47f2-99b5-2d8305ec3b6a"}
