{"type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"tool_use_id":"toolu_01HHJivM5WhiGQkxWgoTKbUa","type":"tool_result","content":[{"type":"text","text":"Review submitted. Here is the summary:\n\n**Score: 3.0 (Findings-level, not Main)**\n\n**Key findings from the review:**\n\n1. **Strongest contribution is C2 (Likert-vs-pairwise gap)**, which is a genuinely actionable methodological insight. The n=500 replication showing ~5x sample efficiency advantage is the paper's best result.\n\n2. **Novelty is incremental.** The counterfactual-rewrite pipeline already exists in prior work (Counterfactual Rhetorical 2025, RATE ICML 2025). Pairwise > Likert sensitivity is known from psychometrics (Kiritchenko & Mohammad 2017). CSD combines known ideas in a new application context.\n\n3. **Critical data integrity concern.** The \"best\" paper version reports 7/9 significant formality combinations and all axes working cleanly, but the actual experimental data (median version) shows only 4/9 significant, register borderline on one judge only, and tone marginal on one judge only. The Gemini-Flash results are particularly discrepant (best: 72.4% most susceptible; median: 56.3% not significant).\n\n4. **Missing related work.** RATE (whose protocol CSD directly adopts), Counterfactual Rhetorical (closest methodological competitor), Soumik 2026, and FairJudge 2026 are all absent from the bibliography despite being in the project's own literature map.\n\n5. **C3 (axis spectrum / prestige dimension) is oversold.** The evidence supports \"formality is the only axis that reliably works\" rather than a systematic decomposition across axes.\n\n6. **Detection without correction limits impact.** Unlike LC-AlpacaEval, CSD provides no mitigation strategy.\n\n**Path to improvement:** Foreground C2 as primary contribution, use honest median-version numbers, add missing citations (especially RATE attribution), reframe C3 as preliminary, and consider adding at least a comparison against LC-AlpacaEval's detection capability."},{"type":"text","text":"agentId: aa1f51bb281c12b77 (use SendMessage with to: 'aa1f51bb281c12b77' to continue this agent)\n<usage>total_tokens: 88802\ntool_uses: 20\nduration_ms: 188154</usage>"}]}]},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"e3a9c93d-dffd-441a-83b0-1831ff4a3a69","uuid":"860c695b-3f19-436d-b8cf-e3ab4c766ba5","timestamp":"2026-05-20T21:59:25.835Z","tool_use_result":{"status":"completed","prompt":"You are Reviewer 2 (R2) for a top ML venue review. Your focus areas are **Novelty/Contribution** and **Related Work/Positioning**. Review the paper below independently and produce a structured review. Use a 6-point scale: 1=Strong Reject, 2=Reject, 3=Weak Reject, 4=Weak Accept, 5=Accept, 6=Strong Accept. Target venue acceptance rate ~25%.\n\n## Paper Summary\n\n**Title**: Causal Style Decomposition: Revealing Axis-Dependent Style Bias in LLM Judges via Counterfactual Pairwise Comparison\n\nThe paper proposes CSD (Causal Style Decomposition), a black-box post-hoc method to detect per-axis causal style bias in LLM judges. It generates binary style counterfactuals (text pairs differing only along one style axis, e.g., formal vs. casual), verifies content preservation via NLI+BERTScore dual gate, and measures bias via position-controlled pairwise comparison.\n\nThree claims:\n- C1 (Detection): Formality bias is significant on 2/3 frontier judges (GPT-4o 69.1% p=0.006, Claude 65.4% p=0.021), judge-dependent in magnitude. Replicates with independent rewriter. Survives original-preference correction on LLMBar.\n- C2 (Measurement): Likert scoring fails at n=100 (p=0.332) while pairwise is significant (p=0.006). At n=500, Likert barely reaches p=0.042 (d=0.097) while pairwise gives p<0.001 (h=0.228). Pairwise is ~5x more sample-efficient.\n- C3 (Axis spectrum): formality=clean, register=borderline (p=0.048 GPT-4o only), tone=marginal (p=0.092 GPT-4o only), verbosity=fail (NLI 65%). Gradient tracks \"prestige\" dimension.\n\n## Method\n\nCSD has 3 stages:\n1. **Binary style counterfactual generation**: Use instruction-tuned LLM (Qwen3-32B primary, Llama-3.1-70B validation) to rewrite text along a single style axis while preserving content.\n2. **Content verification**: Dual-gate NLI (≥0.80) + BERTScore (≥0.90). Only pairs passing both proceed.\n3. **Position-controlled pairwise comparison**: Query judge twice with swapped positions. Only position-consistent pairs counted. Two-tailed sign test.\n\nAlso compute Likert ATE on same data for comparison.\n\n## Related Work (as presented in paper)\n\nThe paper positions against:\n- **Length/style bias literature**: Dubois et al. (2024) length-controlled AlpacaEval; SOS-Bench (2025) style-vs-substance; Raina et al. (2024) self-preference; Preference Leakage (2026) training data bias; CASSE (2026) 12×12 style feature matrix but correlational only.\n- **Causal inference for bias**: Lu et al. (2020) counterfactual for social bias in NLP; CausalBench (2025) interventional benchmarking.\n- **Pairwise vs pointwise**: Kiritchenko & Mohammad (2017) best-worst scaling; Zheng et al. (2024) MT-Bench.\n\nThe paper claims novelty in: (1) applying counterfactual intervention to style bias in LLM judges (vs social bias), (2) systematic pairwise-vs-Likert sensitivity comparison on controlled data, (3) per-axis decomposition.\n\n## Key Results Summary\n\n- Formality bias: 2/3 judges significant on LLMBar, effect gradient LLMBar > AlpacaEval >> MT-Bench\n- 4/9 judge×benchmark combos significant at p<0.05 (only 2 survive original-preference correction)\n- Likert vs Pairwise: All 4 pairwise-significant combos have Likert NS; GPT-4o Likert ATE has wrong direction\n- Register: Only GPT-4o borderline p=0.048, doesn't survive multiple comparison correction\n- Tone: Only GPT-4o marginal p=0.092\n- Verbosity: Content preservation fails, serves as negative control\n- Rewriter robustness: Llama replicates Qwen results (Fisher p=0.812)\n- Scaled replication (n=500): Win rate 61.4% (down from 69.1% at n=100), p<0.001\n\n## Your Review Task\n\nProduce a structured review covering:\n1. **Summary** (2-3 sentences)\n2. **Strengths** (bulleted, focus on novelty, significance of contribution, positioning vs prior work, clarity)\n3. **Weaknesses** (bulleted, focus on novelty concerns, missing related work, overclaiming, framing issues, contribution significance)\n4. **Questions for Authors** (specific, actionable)\n5. **Minor Issues**\n6. **Overall Assessment** (score 1-6 with justification)\n7. **Confidence** (1-5)\n\nKey questions to consider:\n- Is the counterfactual generation + pairwise comparison genuinely novel vs prior work on style bias (CASSE, SOS-Bench)?\n- Is the Likert vs pairwise finding surprising or well-known?\n- Does C3 (axis spectrum) deliver enough beyond \"formality is the only axis that works\"?\n- Is the \"prestige dimension\" interpretation supported or speculative?\n- Are the 10 references sufficient? What major related work is missing?\n- Does the contribution meet the bar for a top venue (acceptance ~25%)?\n\nBe rigorous and specific.","agentId":"aa1f51bb281c12b77","agentType":"general-purpose","content":[{"type":"text","text":"Review submitted. Here is the summary:\n\n**Score: 3.0 (Findings-level, not Main)**\n\n**Key findings from the review:**\n\n1. **Strongest contribution is C2 (Likert-vs-pairwise gap)**, which is a genuinely actionable methodological insight. The n=500 replication showing ~5x sample efficiency advantage is the paper's best result.\n\n2. **Novelty is incremental.** The counterfactual-rewrite pipeline already exists in prior work (Counterfactual Rhetorical 2025, RATE ICML 2025). Pairwise > Likert sensitivity is known from psychometrics (Kiritchenko & Mohammad 2017). CSD combines known ideas in a new application context.\n\n3. **Critical data integrity concern.** The \"best\" paper version reports 7/9 significant formality combinations and all axes working cleanly, but the actual experimental data (median version) shows only 4/9 significant, register borderline on one judge only, and tone marginal on one judge only. The Gemini-Flash results are particularly discrepant (best: 72.4% most susceptible; median: 56.3% not significant).\n\n4. **Missing related work.** RATE (whose protocol CSD directly adopts), Counterfactual Rhetorical (closest methodological competitor), Soumik 2026, and FairJudge 2026 are all absent from the bibliography despite being in the project's own literature map.\n\n5. **C3 (axis spectrum / prestige dimension) is oversold.** The evidence supports \"formality is the only axis that reliably works\" rather than a systematic decomposition across axes.\n\n6. **Detection without correction limits impact.** Unlike LC-AlpacaEval, CSD provides no mitigation strategy.\n\n**Path to improvement:** Foreground C2 as primary contribution, use honest median-version numbers, add missing citations (especially RATE attribution), reframe C3 as preliminary, and consider adding at least a comparison against LC-AlpacaEval's detection capability."}],"totalDurationMs":188154,"totalTokens":88802,"totalToolUseCount":20,"usage":{"input_tokens":1,"cache_creation_input_tokens":3627,"cache_read_input_tokens":84690,"output_tokens":484,"server_tool_use":{"web_search_requests":0,"web_fetch_requests":0},"service_tier":"standard","cache_creation":{"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":0,"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":3627},"inference_geo":"","iterations":[{"input_tokens":1,"output_tokens":484,"cache_read_input_tokens":84690,"cache_creation_input_tokens":3627,"cache_creation":{"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":3627,"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":0},"type":"message"}],"speed":"standard"},"toolStats":{"readCount":7,"searchCount":3,"bashCount":0,"editFileCount":0,"linesAdded":0,"linesRemoved":0,"otherToolCount":10}}}
